      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 The Obesity Society.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To measure long-term changes in resting metabolic rate (RMR) and body composition in participants of \"The Biggest Loser\" competition."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Body composition was measured by dual energy X-ray absorptiometry, and RMR was determined by indirect calorimetry at baseline, at the end of the 30-week competition and 6 years later. Metabolic adaptation was defined as the residual RMR after adjusting for changes in body composition and age."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Of the 16 \"Biggest Loser\" competitors originally investigated, 14 participated in this follow-up study. Weight loss at the end of the competition was (mean\u2009±\u2009SD) 58.3\u2009±\u200924.9 kg (P < 0.0001), and RMR decreased by 610\u2009±\u2009483 kcal/day (P = 0.0004). After 6 years, 41.0\u2009±\u200931.3 kg of the lost weight was regained (P = 0.0002), while RMR was 704\u2009±\u2009427 kcal/day below baseline (P < 0.0001) and metabolic adaptation was -499\u2009±\u2009207 kcal/day (P < 0.0001). Weight regain was not significantly correlated with metabolic adaptation at the competition's end (r\u2009=\u2009-0.1, P = 0.75), but those subjects maintaining greater weight loss at 6 years also experienced greater concurrent metabolic slowing (r = 0.59, P = 0.025)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Metabolic adaptation persists over time and is likely a proportional, but incomplete, response to contemporaneous efforts to reduce body weight."
          }
        ]
      },
